# Entity details grounding

User request: clickable entity details from search, visually distinct MPs and funders. MPs show recent declarations. Funders show total donated by party and top recipients.

Search flows App.svelte effect -> frontend/src/lib/search.ts /api/search -> Vite strips /api -> HTTP /search -> entity_search service -> MP and funder repositories -> Postgres SQLx query! macros. Search rows are static spans. HTTP DTO drops all IDs. MP model already has Parliament numeric ID, but funder model lacks its database UUID. Search funder repository also drops company number. Existing services, repository traits and explicit error enums are precedent. Public models live under exposed/src/lib/domain/models, with inbound wire DTOs. Database queries must use SQLx macros.

Schema: members(id UUID, parliament_member_id positive int unique, name, party_id, party_name, latest_membership_from, is_current_commons). declarations(id UUID, source_declaration_id positive int unique, member_id UUID, category_id, category_name, registration_date nullable timestamptz, fetched_at). funders(id UUID, funder_name exact-name unique, funder_kind nullable text, company_number nullable text). funding_entries(id UUID, source_declaration_id FK, funder_id nullable UUID, amount nullable numeric, currency nullable text, payment_type nullable text).

Preserve duplicate funding occurrences. Do not DISTINCT amounts. Preserve declarations with zero entries or unknown funder/amount. Use exact decimals as JSON strings. Never mix currencies or turn null into GBP/zero. Recent sort registration_date DESC NULLS LAST, source id DESC tie-break. Member parties are latest stored party only, not historical party at donation date. Wording must explain this and avoid implying every declared support payment went directly to the party/MP. Current schema has no historical party data. Do not add migrations for this feature. Funders use exact source names, not resolved aliases.

Existing UI dark black, Manrope, purple accent. EntityIndicator uses icon tooltip buttons; do not nest interactive elements inside links. Search term/strictness stay in URL. Preserve cancellation, keyboard shortcuts, responsive behavior. User should be able to open links/new tabs, refresh, and go Back to original search. Detail empty, error, not-found, cancellation states are required.

Runtime prepared in isolated worktree. Disposable Compose project exposed-entity-details-20261008, postgres on 127.0.0.1:58982, database exposed, user exposed password exposed_local_dev, sslmode disable. All checked-in migrations applied. Role search_path exposed,public. Existing fixtures loaded. frontend npm dependencies installed. Rust macros compile against DATABASE_URL. Tests use sqlx::test temporary DBs. No SQLx offline cache. No Makefile despite README. Project UI driver at frontend/.agents/skills/verify/SKILL.md. Can use isolated DB with host API and Vite for cheaper live proof. Existing frontend tests mock API.

Read relevant source to confirm. Keep solution small, service pattern intact, scope includes API, frontend, meaningful aggregation and browser checks. No nested agents for candidates. Output artifact only; no implementation code edits.
Score 1-5 each: domain and money accuracy; stable shareable identity/navigation; clear usable differentiated UI; existing layering and small public API; meaningful verification and empty/error coverage. Choose maintainer-safe design, graft useful ideas, reject unnecessary abstraction. Require at least two structurally different designs.
# Entity details design

## Usage (caller's view)

A search result's name is an ordinary link to `/members/4514?q=smith&strictness=1` or `/funders/<uuid>?q=smith&strictness=1`. An MP page displays the latest 20 declarations with their funding entries. A funder page displays declared support by recipients' latest recorded party, followed by the top ten recipients within each currency. Every funder page states that parties reflect the latest stored membership, values describe declared support rather than necessarily direct donations, and exact source names define funder identity.

```rust
// Member HTTP handler, after parsing the path into MemberId.
let details = state.entity_details_service.member(member_id).await?;
Ok(ApiSuccess::new(StatusCode::OK, MemberDetailsResponse::from(details)))

// Funder HTTP handler, after parsing the path into FunderId.
let details = state.entity_details_service.funder(funder_id).await?;
Ok(ApiSuccess::new(StatusCode::OK, FunderDetailsResponse::from(details)))
```

```svelte
<!-- Indicator button remains outside the name anchor. -->
<EntityIndicator {entity} />
<a href={entityHref(entity, searchParams)}>{entity.name}</a>
<!-- A recipient on a funder page links to the member page in the same way. -->
```

## Problem

Search currently discards stable identifiers at the HTTP boundary, and `Funder` does not retain its database UUID. The source schema permits repeated funding occurrences, missing values, and declarations without funding entries. The new read path must preserve those facts while keeping aggregation exact and keeping existing search behavior intact. Existing domain services, repository traits, SQLx macros, and explicit errors establish the integration pattern.

## Shape

Use a dedicated details service with two methods and one dedicated read repository. The repository loads bounded member declarations and funder totals grouped by recipient and currency. The service derives party totals and recipient rankings from the same recipient aggregates. No raw payment collection crosses the repository boundary for funder pages, and there is one source for both displayed summaries.

```rust
// domain/models/funder.rs: preserve the existing model, add persistent identity.
pub struct FunderId(Uuid); // FromStr validates at the HTTP boundary.
pub struct Funder { id: FunderId, name: String, funder_kind: FunderKind }
// Update constructors and all current callers. Never fabricate identity.
// Reuse MemberId; add its checked path parser and accessor rather than a second ID.

// domain/models/entity_details.rs: fields shown for design; implement existing getter style.
pub struct MemberDetails {
    member: MemberProfile,
    declarations: Vec<RecentDeclaration>,
    declaration_limit: usize,
}
pub struct MemberProfile { id: MemberId, name: String, party: RecordedParty, is_current_commons: bool }
pub struct RecordedParty { id: PartyId, name: String }
pub struct RecentDeclaration {
    source_id: i64, category_name: String,
    registered_at: Option<DateTime<Utc>>, entries: Vec<DeclaredFunding>,
}
pub struct DeclaredFunding {
    funder: Option<FunderReference>, amount: Option<BigDecimal>,
    currency: Option<String>, payment_type: Option<String>,
}
pub struct FunderReference { id: FunderId, name: String }
pub struct FunderProfile { funder: Funder, company_number: Option<String> }
pub struct FunderDetails {
    profile: FunderProfile, currencies: Vec<CurrencyBreakdown>,
    entry_count: u64, unvalued_entry_count: u64,
}
pub struct CurrencyBreakdown {
    currency: String, parties: Vec<PartyTotal>, top_recipients: Vec<RecipientTotal>,
}
pub struct PartyTotal { party: RecordedParty, amount: BigDecimal }
pub struct RecipientTotal { member: MemberProfile, amount: BigDecimal }
// Read repository result, not an HTTP DTO. One row per recipient/currency.
pub struct FunderFunding { profile: FunderProfile, allocations: Vec<RecipientAllocation> }
pub struct RecipientAllocation {
    member: MemberProfile, currency: Option<String>, known_total: Option<BigDecimal>,
    entry_count: u64, unvalued_entry_count: u64,
}

// domain/repositories/entity_details.rs
pub trait EntityDetailsRepo: Clone + Send + Sync + 'static {
    fn member(&self, id: MemberId, recent_limit: usize)
        -> impl Future<Output = Result<Option<MemberDetails>, EntityDetailsRepoError>> + Send;
    fn funder_funding(&self, id: FunderId)
        -> impl Future<Output = Result<Option<FunderFunding>, EntityDetailsRepoError>> + Send;
}
// domain/services/entity_details.rs, interface/error submodules as existing precedent.
pub trait EntityDetailsService: Clone + Send + Sync + 'static {
    fn member(&self, id: MemberId)
        -> impl Future<Output = Result<MemberDetails, EntityDetailsError>> + Send;
    fn funder(&self, id: FunderId)
        -> impl Future<Output = Result<FunderDetails, EntityDetailsError>> + Send;
}
pub struct Service<R> { repo: R }
fn summarize_funder(funding: FunderFunding) -> FunderDetails { todo!() }
pub enum EntityDetailsError { NotFound, UnexpectedError(String) }
```

`BigDecimal` is a direct domain dependency, matched to SQLx's version rather than imported from `sqlx::types`. SQLx already enables its decimal support. HTTP DTOs serialize decimal values as strings; browser code never converts them to JavaScript numbers. Format grouping with string operations, retain exact fractional digits, and show the source currency text.

`outbound/entity_details.rs` owns SQL and database-row conversion. Member retrieval selects 20 declaration IDs before joining funding entries. Order by registration date descending with nulls last, then source ID descending; order entries by their UUID for stability. Left joins retain declarations without entries and entries without a funder. A missing entry UUID means no entry; null amount never means no entry. Read member existence and details in one SQL statement or one read transaction with a consistent snapshot.

Funder retrieval joins only its own funding occurrences to declarations and members, then groups by recipient ID and source currency. `SUM(amount)` uses every occurrence, never `DISTINCT`. A known total exists only when currency is non-null and at least one amount is known. Unknown currencies never produce a sum because they may represent different currencies. Count an entry as unvalued once when amount or currency is missing. Keep the existing funder row even with no entries, so absence is different from an empty result. No new migration or index is required.

The service owns the 20-declaration and ten-recipient limits, not client options. Its pure funder summarizer groups known currency totals by recorded party ID and ranks recipients independently within each currency by decimal descending, name, then member ID. It adds occurrence and unvalued counts over all rows before truncation. Render no ranking for unknown currencies and explain the unvalued count. Negative and zero reported amounts remain reported amounts. This avoids invented conversions and retains source meaning.

The service hides summary construction and not-found policy behind two calls. The repository hides relational joins and aggregation behind domain results. HTTP only parses IDs and maps domain outcomes to 200, 404, invalid-request, or the existing sanitized 500 response. This keeps the service useful rather than a forwarding wrapper, per boundary-discipline and minimize-reader-load.

`AppState<E, D>` adds the details service; `server.rs` wires it alongside search. `http/handlers/entity_details.rs` owns the new response DTOs. Routes are `/members/{id}` and `/funders/{id}`. Search preserves its existing fields and adds an `id` string, interpreted together with `kind`; the frontend parser produces a discriminated MP/Funder union. Funder repository search selects its UUID and every model constructor receives it.

Frontend ownership is small: `App.svelte` selects the route from `location.pathname`; the existing search screen moves intact into `SearchPage.svelte`; `EntityDetails.svelte` owns request state and layout; `entity-details.ts` owns URL parsing, typed fetches, response parsing, and exact decimal display. Use ordinary full-document links instead of introducing a client router. Browser Back, reload, modifier-click, and new tabs then work natively. Copy search parameters into detail links and derive the explicit Back to search URL from them. Require the host's existing SPA fallback for direct paths; verify it in the running app.

Details use a discriminated state of loading, ready member, ready funder, not found, and error. Abort on teardown and ignore obsolete request completion. Retry starts a new request. A 200 result with empty declarations or currency groups is a ready state with clear empty copy. MP pages have party/member identity and a dated declaration list. Funders have legal-kind identity, company number when available, and tabular currency summaries. Preserve dark Manrope styling; use labels and layout as well as distinct accent colors. Keep all tooltip buttons outside anchors.

## Tradeoffs accepted

- We accept full-document detail navigation for native browser semantics and no routing dependency.
- We accept transferring every recipient/currency aggregate for one funder so party totals and rankings share one input. The transfer is bounded by recipients and currencies, not payment history.
- We accept a fixed recent-declaration window and fixed top-ten ranking for this feature. The interface labels these limits rather than implying completeness.

## Alternatives considered

- Separate repository calls for party totals and top recipients hide more SQL but duplicate grouping rules and can disagree when records change between calls. The service would become a pass-through layer with little ownership.
- Load every funding occurrence and aggregate in the service. This makes ownership simple but puts unbounded historical data into application memory. Recipient/currency aggregation keeps the same public capability with far less transfer.
- A client router with a generic polymorphic entity endpoint saves document reloads but adds navigation state and transport dispatch beyond the two required pages.

## Synthesis decision

Candidate only; the orchestrator selects the base and records adaptations.

## Implementation reconciliation

No implementation deviations yet. The scalar limits and precise response field names can be reconciled without changing ownership or outcomes.

## Open questions and risks

Can production serve the frontend entry point on direct member and funder paths? Verify this before shipping; if it cannot, use query-based detail URLs with the same native navigation policy.

## Next implementation step

Add persistent funder identity and the dedicated details repository, then prove aggregation against fixtures containing duplicate values, multiple currencies, missing amounts/currencies, empty declarations, unknown funders, exact decimals, and date ties before wiring the service and pages.
# Entity details candidate

## Usage (caller's view)

Search results expose stable identity: MPs use Parliament's numeric ID; funders use their stored UUID. An MP name links to `/members/123?q=John&strictness=1`, a funder name to `/funders/<uuid>?q=John&strictness=1`. These are ordinary anchors supporting refresh, new tabs, and browser Back. Each detail page also provides a search link carrying only `q` and `strictness`.

```rust
// HTTP handlers parse path values into domain IDs, then request one complete page.
let details = state.entity_details_service.member(member_id).await?;
Ok(ApiSuccess::new(StatusCode::OK, MemberDetailsResponse::from(details)))
let details = state.entity_details_service.funder(funder_id).await?;
Ok(ApiSuccess::new(StatusCode::OK, FunderDetailsResponse::from(details)))
```

```ts
// DetailPage.svelte owns its request lifetime; transport never returns unchecked JSON.
const details = await getMemberDetails(route.memberId, controller.signal);
// Funder rendering traverses currencies first: each has its own party totals/ranking.
for (const section of details.currencies) renderCurrencySection(section);
```

## Problem

The search UI currently drops entity IDs and has no detail navigation. Stored declarations contain nullable dates and funding facts; monetary totals must retain exact amounts, duplicate occurrences, and currency distinctions. Party attribution is the member's latest stored affiliation, not affiliation when support was declared. Existing service/repository/error boundaries and compile-checked SQLx queries remain the integration pattern. No schema changes are needed.

## Shape

Introduce a single `entity_details` feature with two typed service operations. Their output types intentionally differ: MPs expose recent declarations; funders expose aggregates. Do not force both into a sparse bag of optional fields.

```rust
// domain/models/entity_details.rs: conceptual field sketches; private fields + accessors.
struct ParliamentMemberId(NonZeroU32); // additionally constrained to PostgreSQL i32 range
struct FunderId(Uuid);                // parsed once at HTTP boundary
struct MemberDetails {
    member: ParliamentMember,
    declarations: Vec<RecentDeclaration>,
}
struct RecentDeclaration {
    source_id: i32,
    category_name: String,
    registered_at: Option<DateTime<Utc>>,
    entries: Vec<DeclaredFunding>,
}
struct DeclaredFunding {
    funder: Option<FunderReference>,
    amount: Option<BigDecimal>,
    currency: Option<String>,
    payment_type: Option<String>,
}
struct FunderReference { id: FunderId, name: String }
struct FunderDetails {
    funder: Funder, // extend existing model with required FunderId + optional company number
    currencies: Vec<CurrencySummary>,
}
struct CurrencySummary {
    currency: Option<String>, // None remains an explicit “Currency unrecorded” section
    parties: Vec<PartyTotal>,
    top_recipients: Vec<RecipientTotal>,
}
struct FundingTotal {
    known_amount: Option<BigDecimal>, // SQL SUM; None if all source amounts unknown
    entry_count: u64,
    unknown_amount_count: u64,
}
struct PartyTotal { party_id: i32, party_name: String, total: FundingTotal }
struct RecipientTotal { member: ParliamentMember, total: FundingTotal }

trait EntityDetailsService: Clone + Send + Sync + 'static {
    fn member(&self, id: ParliamentMemberId)
        -> impl Future<Output = Result<MemberDetails, EntityDetailsError>> + Send;
    fn funder(&self, id: FunderId)
        -> impl Future<Output = Result<FunderDetails, EntityDetailsError>> + Send;
}
enum EntityDetailsError { NotFound, UnexpectedError(String) }
struct Service<R> { repository: R }
// Bodies: not implemented; service owns page limits, not-found policy and error adaptation.
trait EntityDetailsRepo: Clone + Send + Sync + 'static {
    fn get_member(&self, id: ParliamentMemberId, recent_limit: u8)
        -> impl Future<Output = Result<Option<MemberDetails>, EntityDetailsRepoError>> + Send;
    fn get_funder(&self, id: FunderId, recipient_limit_per_currency: u8)
        -> impl Future<Output = Result<Option<FunderDetails>, EntityDetailsRepoError>> + Send;
}
enum EntityDetailsRepoError { DatabaseError(String), InvalidStoredData(String) }
```

The complete-page interface hides joins, null handling, grouping and limit policy. Its service boundary remains useful for fixed page policy, typed absence and existing app composition; it must not grow additional orchestration layers. Domain read models carry no Axum, Serde or SQLx row types (boundary-discipline, minimize-reader-load).

`MemberId` already exists but lacks parsing/accessors; prefer evolving that existing wrapper into the typed `ParliamentMemberId` contract rather than keeping two wrappers with equivalent meaning. Expose the same ID accessor to search DTO conversion. Required funder identity goes on existing `Funder`, because every current caller represents a stored search funder; update its constructor and tests in the same wave. Do not manufacture identities or add a name-based detail fallback.

### Storage and aggregate contract

- `outbound/entity_details.rs` implements the repository with `query!` macros and explicit fallible row conversion. Use `sqlx::types::BigDecimal` and the SQLx-enabled timestamp representation at this adapter; convert timestamps to existing Chrono domain conventions.
- Member identity query distinguishes absent member from an existing member with no declarations. Select the most recent 10 declarations before joining entries: registration date DESC NULLS LAST, source ID DESC. Read entries separately for those declaration IDs, or join the limited CTE with funding entries. Preserve empty declarations and every entry; order entries by stored UUID for deterministic output.
- Funder identity query distinguishes absent funder from no linked entries. Aggregate funding entries joined through declarations to members, filtered by the exact funder UUID. Group by currency plus party `(party_id, party_name)` for totals, and currency plus member ID for recipients. Never use `DISTINCT`, monetary floats, currency defaults or `COALESCE(SUM(amount), 0)`.
- SQL `SUM(amount)`, `COUNT(*)`, and `COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE amount IS NULL)` provide each total. Unknown amounts are excluded from the sum and their counts remain visible. Null currency gets its own bucket; display its known-number sum with an explicit missing-currency label.
- Rank recipients separately per currency, known sum DESC NULLS LAST, Parliament member ID ASC as tie-break. Include at most 10 per currency. Never publish an overall winner across currencies. Party rows use the same deterministic order within each currency.
- A short read-only repeatable-read transaction encloses identity and detail queries, so an import cannot split one page across snapshots. This is request-local state, not a cache or shared lock.
- “By party” means support declared by members grouped by their latest stored party. Display that explanation next to the totals. Preserve source funder names without alias resolution. Use “Declared support” and “Top declaring MPs” rather than asserting direct payments to parties.

### Module ownership and HTTP

`domain/models/entity_details.rs` owns IDs/read models; `domain/repositories/entity_details.rs` owns the storage port; `domain/services/entity_details/{interface,error}.rs` plus its parent own page policy. `outbound/entity_details.rs` owns SQL and projection. Existing `inbound/http/handlers.rs` owns response DTOs and routes add `GET /members/{id}` and `GET /funders/{id}`. Extend `AppState<E,D>` and server wiring with the detail service; keep the search service separate.

Add `ApiError::NotFound` mapped to 404. Malformed numeric/UUID paths yield client errors; absent valid IDs yield 404; unexpected storage errors yield the existing generic 500. Search DTOs gain a discriminated identity: MP `member_id`, funder `funder_id`; frontend parsing requires the matching field. Do not expose member database UUIDs.

DTO amount fields are decimal strings or null. Frontend amount formatting operates on decimal strings and preserves precision; it must not convert to JavaScript Number merely to call Intl.NumberFormat. Do not change existing search response field conventions gratuitously.

### Frontend

Move the existing search view into `SearchPage.svelte` with its debounce, URL updates, shortcuts, strictness and cancellation intact. `App.svelte` retains shared shell and chooses SearchPage or separate MemberDetails/FunderDetails views from pathname. Ordinary anchor navigation uses browser history; no client router library is necessary. Ensure deployment serves the app for both detail path families; Vite already supports development fallback.

Search result links wrap the entity name only; `EntityIndicator` tooltip buttons remain siblings. Visually distinguish MP and funder headers with explicit labels and existing Parliament/coin icons, retaining dark backgrounds, Manrope and purple accent. MP content is a dated declaration list with nested funding facts; funder content is currency sections containing party totals and ranked recipients. Recipient and known-funder names are navigable using the same search context.

`lib/entity-details.ts` owns response parsing and abortable fetches. Each detail component has a discriminated loading/ready/not-found/error state, cleanup abort, stale-response guard, timeout and retry. Cancellation must not become an error. Empty declarations and empty funder aggregates are ready states with clear empty messages; missing dates, amounts and funders have explicit labels. Search-only keyboard handlers live with SearchPage, avoiding references to an absent input.

## Synthesis decision

Candidate only; the orchestrator records the chosen base and grafts after comparison.

## Tradeoffs accepted

- Accept native document navigation in exchange for correct Back/new-tab/refresh behavior without introducing a routing framework.
- Accept per-currency recipient lists in exchange for meaningful ranking without conversion rates or mixed-currency totals.
- Accept one thin service policy boundary in exchange for preserving existing architecture and future caller consistency.

## Alternatives considered

- A generic tagged `/entities/{kind}/{id}` page API reduces route count but exposes entity branching to all callers and encourages nullable MP/funder fields. Two complete-page operations hide each feature's distinct knowledge better.
- Returning every funder funding entry and aggregating in the browser gives the API a tiny surface but makes every consumer responsible for decimal arithmetic, null coverage, currency separation and party semantics, while transferring unbounded history.
- A SPA router with intercepted anchors hides navigation mechanics but adds history and modifier-key policy the native browser already implements; revisit when several more page types need persistent in-memory state.

## Implementation reconciliation

No implementation yet. Record accepted changes here and reconcile affected contracts before handoff.

## Open questions and risks

Can the existing production frontend host provide pathname fallback? If not, choose hash paths consistently before building links; do not ship links that only work under Vite. Are ten recent declarations and ten recipients per currency sufficient for the first feature? Treat these as explicit initial limits, with no pagination claimed.

## Next implementation step

Implement ID propagation and repository projections, then prove duplicate entries, empty declarations, null amounts/dates/currencies, per-currency ranking, exact decimal serialization and missing IDs with SQLx integration fixtures before wiring the pages and checking native navigation in the live browser.
# Entity details design candidate

## Problem

Search currently returns names and kind labels only, so rows cannot navigate to stable detail pages. MPs already have a Parliament ID; funders do not expose their database UUID. The feature needs two distinct views over declarations and funding entries while preserving repeated source occurrences, incomplete records, exact numeric values, and existing domain/service/repository boundaries. The latest stored MP party is the only party association available; it must be labeled as current/latest and never presented as historical donation affiliation. No schema migration is needed.

## Usage (caller's view)

A search result carries a stable route key and becomes a normal link; opening or refreshing the URL works independently of search state:

```svelte
<a href={entity.kind === "MP" ? `/members/${entity.memberId}` : `/funders/${entity.funderId}`}>
  <EntityIndicator {entity} /><span>{entity.name}</span>
</a>
```

The detail page loads by route key and owns loading, not-found, error, and success states:

```ts
const result = await getMemberDetails(Number(params.memberId), signal);
// MemberDetails: identity + latest party + recent declarations with all entries
```

```ts
const result = await getFunderDetails(params.funderId, signal);
// FunderDetails: exact source name + totals grouped by latest member party and currency
// + top recipients grouped by member and currency
```

Both detail loaders use ordinary GETs and AbortSignal; clicking Back returns to the prior search URL (`q` and `strictness` remain intact).

## Shape

Add a focused `entity_details` service and repository rather than widening search into a multi-purpose service. Public domain data is detail-shaped: `MemberDetails { member: ParliamentMember, declarations: Vec<RecentDeclaration> }`, `RecentDeclaration { source_id: NonZeroU32, category: String, registered_at: Option<DateTime<Utc>>, entries: Vec<DeclaredEntry> }`, and `DeclaredEntry { funder_name: Option<String>, amount: Option<Decimal>, currency: Option<String>, payment_type: Option<String> }`. Preserve every entry row and declarations with no entries; order by `registered_at DESC NULLS LAST, source_id DESC`. Use `rust_decimal::Decimal` in domain and serialize amounts as JSON strings only in HTTP DTOs.

`FunderDetails { funder: FunderIdentity { id: Uuid, name: String, kind: Option<String>, company_number: Option<String> }, totals_by_party: Vec<PartyTotal>, top_recipients: Vec<RecipientTotal> }`; totals carry `party_name` (nullable), `currency` (nullable), occurrence count, known-amount count, and `total: Option<Decimal>`. Recipient totals carry member ID/name, latest stored party label, currency, occurrence/known counts and optional exact total. These are grouped by `funding_entries` occurrences; NULL amount is excluded from the sum and counted as unknown, while NULL currency remains its own group. Never DISTINCT amounts or coalesce null currency to GBP. Use deterministic ordering and a bounded top-N constant (e.g. 10), including ties resolved by total descending then Parliament ID/currency.

Add `domain/models/entity_details.rs`, `domain/repositories/entity_details_repository.rs`, `domain/services/entity_details.rs` (+ interface/error), and implement SQLx `query!`/`query_as!` methods in `outbound/entity_details.rs`. Keep SQL at this adapter; two methods return typed raw row collections, and service assembles nested declaration groups / aggregates into domain models. Simpler option: let repository return already grouped domain details; reject because it moves business/domain assembly into SQL-specific code and makes alternate repositories awkward. The service owns `EntityDetailsService::member_details(NonZeroU32, limit)` and `funder_details(Uuid, top_n)`; repository exposes corresponding typed lookups and explicit database errors. Missing row is `Ok(None)`; malformed database values and SQL failures are repository errors.

Add GET `/members/{member_id}` and `/funders/{funder_id}` handlers. Parse path values at the HTTP boundary into `NonZeroU32` / `Uuid`; invalid route keys return 400, missing entities 404, DB failures 500. DTOs mirror the two domain views but convert Decimal to canonical base-10 strings; names, category and optional metadata remain plain data. Extend search response with `member_id` for MPs and `funder_id` UUID for funders; maintain a tagged optional-key invariant via a Rust enum (`SearchEntityResponse::Member { member_id, ... } | Funder { funder_id, ... }`) instead of two independently nullable identifiers.

Frontend `Entity` becomes a discriminated union with stable ID. `search.ts` validates the matching ID per kind. Add route-aware detail page components/API functions; retain search as the home view and preserve URL query parameters. Use visually distinct MP and funder page headers/cards consistent with existing palette. Interactive result rows are anchors containing indicator/name content; never nest EntityIndicator buttons inside anchors. Browser back/forward, new tab, direct URL, abort cancellation, responsive layout, and all loading/empty/error/not-found states are explicit behavior contracts.

Interface depth is small: one service, one repository, two GETs, and two page loaders conceal joins, grouping, row assembly, and wire conversion. The API exposes only stable path IDs and detail facts; it does not expose SQL rows or transport types to the frontend.

## Synthesis decision

Candidate proposes per-entity detail routes backed by one detail service/repository. Arena synthesis pending.

## Tradeoffs accepted

- We accept an additional service/repository pair in exchange for keeping search focused and detail aggregation behind one capability.
- We accept nullable currency and amount groups in exchange for faithful representation of source uncertainty.
- We accept latest-party grouping as a descriptive current-data view in exchange for working without unavailable historical party data; UI copy must state the limitation.
- We accept a bounded top-recipient list and deterministic ties in exchange for predictable payload size.

## Alternatives considered

- A single `/entities/{kind}/{id}` endpoint: it shares route handling but exposes kind-dependent ID parsing and response variants throughout the frontend, with no reduction in underlying data complexity; two explicit routes make identity and not-found behavior clearer.
- Load declarations/entries directly in the HTTP handlers: it shortens the type count but exposes database assembly and aggregation to transport code, weakening test seams and existing service ownership.
- Return raw funding entries and aggregate in Svelte: this exposes unbounded row volume and currency/unknown-value rules to the client. The selected design hides those rules and returns compact exact aggregates.

## Implementation reconciliation

None yet. Update the caller usage and affected signatures here if implementation changes the accepted contract, with the accepting source.

## Open questions and risks

- Should top recipients mean sum of known amounts per recipient, with null amount occurrences shown only as counts? This sketch uses that definition and keeps unknown counts visible.
- Should zero-known-amount aggregate groups display a blank total rather than numeric zero? The contract uses `None` when no amount is known.
- Search limits remain per type; stable IDs must be selected in the existing SQL queries and not inferred from names.

## Next implementation step

Implement typed domain detail models and repository/service signatures first, then write SQLx queries that preserve declaration and funding-entry row cardinality.

